1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,700
Here we are, welcome to this new in-depth analysis. Today's title poses a question as

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:09,980
simple as it is uncomfortable: why do reforms cost votes?

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:14,339
Today we are going to explore one of the most incredible paradoxes of modern politics.

4
00:00:14,339 --> 00:00:19,339
We will analyze a truly monumental database to try to uncover a mystery, namely why

5
00:00:19,339 --> 00:00:24,660
doing the objectively right thing for the economy almost always turns out to be a lethal move for

6
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:29,579
the career of those who govern. In short, fasten your seatbelts because we are diving into the data.

7
00:00:29,579 --> 00:00:35,259
And to understand how deeply rooted this problem is, we have to take a big leap back in time.

8
00:00:35,259 --> 00:00:40,500
Just think, Machiavelli had already warned us in 1513, writing that there is

9
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,539
literally nothing more dangerous than setting out to introduce a new order of things.

10
00:00:45,539 --> 00:00:50,740
Because, well, because the innovator makes enemies of all those who benefited from the old system and finds

11
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:54,980
very, very lukewarm defenders in those who might benefit from the new.

12
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:00,020
This resistance to change is not just philosophy; it is a centuries-old problem that today

13
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,660
we see exploding in full force in our democracies.

14
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:08,579
Which brings us straight to our fundamental question, the literal, true mystery:

15
00:01:08,579 --> 00:01:13,260
why does trying to improve the economy in the long term so often cost a

16
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:17,700
political leader their job? I mean, in theory, if a government manages the economy well, voters should

17
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:22,420
reward it, right? Even classical economic theory says exactly that. And yet, when real

18
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:26,579
structural reforms are put in place, the leaders who take responsibility for them are

19
00:01:26,579 --> 00:01:31,379
regularly punished at the ballot box. There is a sort of crazy short-circuit between good intentions

20
00:01:31,379 --> 00:01:36,820
for the country and electoral support. Now, to answer this question we won't be here just

21
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:41,680
making idle chit-chat, but we will rely on a literal mountain of data. A team of

22
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:47,260
top-tier researchers created the Structural Reform Database, which is basically

23
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:52,140
the most massive database ever built on this topic. We are talking about something that covers

24
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:59,340
90 nations from 1973 to 2014; they analyzed reforms in every sector and crossed it all with

25
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:04,780
the elections of 66 democracies. In short, it is not a theoretical exercise; it is the economic

26
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:10,099
and electoral history of half the world, put under the microscope. Just to be

27
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:16,140
clear and keep our analysis completely neutral, what exactly do we mean by reform?

28
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:21,900
The researchers used a very simple scale from 0 to 1. 0 means maximum regulation,

29
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:27,100
1 means complete liberalization. So, when we say reform in this context,

30
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:31,940
we simply mean a movement towards the free market—no politics, no ideological

31
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:35,580
preference, we only measure the extent of the change, period.

32
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:41,700
And here, here we get to the heart of the problem. It's all a matter of out-of-sync timing. Imagine

33
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:46,820
two clocks ticking on different planets. On one side you have the economic clock,

34
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:51,700
which is extremely slow. If you pass a reform in the first year, the costs arrive immediately between the

35
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:57,020
second and third year. The benefits? Those keep you waiting until the fourth or fifth year.

36
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:06,580
On the other hand, however, you have the political clock running wildly towards the next

37
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:11,260
elections. You see the madness. The politician has to ask for votes precisely when the citizens

38
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:15,819
are paying the bill for the reform, but don't see its fruits yet. To understand this

39
00:03:15,819 --> 00:03:21,340
better, look at the effect on GDP, gross domestic product. When a major

40
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:25,539
reform starts, in the short term, the effect is practically zero, flat. And only from the fourth, and even

41
00:03:25,539 --> 00:03:30,219
more clearly from the fifth year, do we see an increase in economic output, let's say

42
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:35,219
around one percent. It's a good result, sure. But let's put ourselves in the shoes of those

43
00:03:35,219 --> 00:03:40,939
governing. Five years of waiting, in politics, is equivalent to about two or three

44
00:03:40,939 --> 00:03:45,620
geological eras. So, someone might say, "Okay, but if liberalizations take

45
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:50,579
so long, I'll do the opposite: I'll put rules back in place and gain support right away."

46
00:03:50,579 --> 00:03:55,219
If only it were that easy. The data slaps us in the face with a terrible truth. It is true

47
00:03:55,219 --> 00:04:00,620
that the benefits of liberalizations are slow, but the damage caused by introducing new

48
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:05,500
restrictions is immediate. Blocking or reversing a reform immediately causes economic output to crash.

49
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:09,620
Practically, it is a trap with no way out. If you push for reforms,

50
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:17,300
you lose votes in the short term. If you go backward, you cause short-term economic damage.

51
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:22,579
It is a literally devastating asymmetry. On one hand, we have a slow, gradual,

52
00:04:22,579 --> 00:04:28,100
arduous climb that slowly builds growth. On the other hand, when regulations are tightened,

53
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:32,460
we have a dizzying and very fast collapse. Just like in life, destroying is much, much

54
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:37,300
faster than building. And for those who have to govern while trying to balance the state's

55
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:43,100
books with the desperate need not to get kicked out in the next elections,

56
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:43,100
well, it's a nightmare. And let's quantify this nightmare. What actually happens? Numbers in hand,

57
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:48,379
if you force a liberal reform exactly in the election year, here is the answer. You suffer

58
00:04:48,379 --> 00:04:55,580
a sharp drop of 1.4% in your vote share. Now, 1.4% might not seem like

59
00:04:55,579 --> 00:05:00,300
the end of the world, right? But in a competitive system where governments are decided on a

60
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:05,459
razor's edge, losing one and a half percentage points means packing up and clearing out

61
00:05:05,459 --> 00:05:10,579
the office. It's a very heavy price to pay for doing the right thing at the wrong time.

62
00:05:10,579 --> 00:05:16,339
But then is there no hope? Actually, there is a highly strategic way out: timing.

63
00:05:16,339 --> 00:05:21,379
The data is clear. Passing reforms close to an election is political suicide.

64
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:26,019
But the memory of voters, let's face it, is surprisingly short. If the reform is

65
00:05:26,019 --> 00:05:30,939
approved at the beginning of the term, that electoral cost practically vanishes. The hit comes

66
00:05:30,939 --> 00:05:36,659
immediately, voters get angry, but after three or four years, not only has the anger faded,

67
00:05:36,659 --> 00:05:40,579
but the first concrete benefits on the economy also begin to show.

68
00:05:40,579 --> 00:05:45,459
Imagine the situation as if it were a very bitter medicine. Structural reforms,

69
00:05:45,459 --> 00:05:50,659
like those on the labor market, act a bit like a very strong treatment. Even before

70
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:55,580
making you better, they knock you down. Unemployment might rise temporarily, wages

71
00:05:55,580 --> 00:06:00,740
have to adjust. In short, future benefits are widespread, but present pains are

72
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:05,580
strongly localized and make a lot of noise. So if you go to the patient while they are

73
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:10,340
writhing in pain from the treatment and ask for their opinion on the doctor, well, of course they'll want to

74
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:15,620
fire them instantly. But watch out, because there is another plot twist that completely

75
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:20,579
changes the game. And that is the general state of health of the economy when

76
00:06:20,579 --> 00:06:25,420
the reform is introduced. The effect is not always the same. Researchers found

77
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:30,579
that the exact same reform can spark diametrically opposite reactions depending on whether we launch it while

78
00:06:30,579 --> 00:06:35,300
the economy is soaring or while it is sinking. Context, when it comes to the patience

79
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:41,459
of voters, is literally everything. And here is the golden rule that emerges: making reforms

80
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:46,740
during a recession is the best way to maximize the electoral penalty. It is

81
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:51,260
a catastrophe. Making layoffs easier at a time when jobs are already scarce

82
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:57,620
only worsens the crisis and infuriates people. But if you pass that exact same

83
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:02,820
reform during a period of economic expansion, not only do they forgive you, but

84
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:08,060
sometimes you are even rewarded. To return to our metaphor, if you give strong medicine

85
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:13,259
to a patient who is already half dead, it ends badly. If you give it to them when they are in good shape, it works

86
00:07:13,259 --> 00:07:19,699
great. Who is at risk of getting hurt the absolute most? The research leaves no doubt.

87
00:07:19,699 --> 00:07:25,420
Single-party governments and majoritarian systems, especially in rich countries. Why?

88
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:30,060
Very simple: accountability. If you are in a large coalition that's a bit of a

89
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:36,100
mixed bag, blame gets diluted and passed around, but in a majoritarian system, there is only one

90
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:41,180
clear, bright target. Voters know exactly who is in front of them on the ballot

91
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:46,660
and know very well who to punish. In short, all this brings us to a rather big provocation

92
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:51,780
that I want you to think about. If our democratic system itself mathematically punishes

93
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:56,100
anyone trying to create long-term growth during difficult times,

94
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:00,500
then we are fundamentally discouraging political courage. In other words, is our desire for

95
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:05,300
immediate results perhaps condemning us to stay stagnant? Do we want politicians who calculate

96
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:10,100
every move just to survive the next vote, or leaders willing to take the flak today

97
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:15,300
to save the next generations? It is a huge question, and I'll leave you to find the answer.

98
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:15,939
Until next time!